emu68 Driver Stack @PACKAGE_VERSION@
========================
AmigaOS drivers for Raspberry Pi 4 / Compute Module 4 running Emu68.

*** UPGRADING FROM 2.0? Two things can change how your machine comes up.
*** Network interfaces are no longer configured in ENVARC:netstack.prefs -
*** each one now has its own file in DEVS:NetInterfaces/ - so a fixed address
*** set up under 2.0 comes back up on DHCP until you re-enter it there; the
*** Install script warns you when it sees an old prefs file. And NVMe
*** partition numbering can shift, because exFAT partitions now mount too.
*** Keep a bootable backup of LIBS: and DEVS: before installing.


THIS ARCHIVE
------------

  @DEBUG_BACKEND@
  @DEBUG_BACKEND_DESCRIPTION@

  @CACHE_OPS_DESCRIPTION@


WHAT TO INSTALL
---------------

The one thing that decides everything: does your Emu68 have the dcache
extensions? They are not part of official Emu68 - they are a separate change to
its JIT that has not been merged upstream, so having them means running a
custom Emu68 build:

  https://github.com/rondoval/Emu68/releases/tag/v1.1-alpha-with-rangeops

Without them the drivers still work, but the cache housekeeping around every
transfer has to be done the slow way - and the bundled TCP/IP stack, which is
built around that housekeeping being cheap, ends up SLOWER than the plain old
SANA-II driver. So on official Emu68 there is no point installing the netdev
Ethernet driver at all: take the standard archive and pair the SANA-II driver
with Roadshow, AmiTCP or Miami. On a custom build with the extensions, take the
'-rangeops' archive and the netdev driver with the bundled lwip-amiga stack,
which gets you close to gigabit line rate. USB is a separate, independent
question: it depends only on which Poseidon you run.

The installer asks two separate questions - which genet.device, and which
TCP/IP stack. They are independent now: since its 1.4 release the bundled
lwip-amiga stack drives classic SANA-II Ethernet drivers as well as the
netdev one, so the SANA-II genet works with either stack.

Find your row:

  Emu68            Poseidon  Archive             Ethernet drv  TCP/IP     USB
  ---------------  --------  ------------------  ------------  ---------  ---
  official 1.1a1+  4.x       <ver>.lha           SANA-II 3.x   any        5.x
  official 1.1a1+  6.x       <ver>.lha           SANA-II 3.x   any        6.x
  custom+dcache    4.x       <ver>-rangeops.lha  netdev 4.x    bundled    5.x
  custom+dcache    6.x       <ver>-rangeops.lha  netdev 4.x    bundled    6.x
  
  official 1.1a1+  Emu68 1.1 alpha.1 or newer, official build
  custom+dcache    a custom Emu68 build carrying the dcache extensions
  4.x / 6.x        classic Poseidon 4.x / Poseidon for AmigaOS 6.x
  <ver>.lha        emu68-drivers-@PACKAGE_VERSION@.lha
  any              your existing Roadshow/AmiTCP/Miami, or the bundled stack

INSTALLING THE BUNDLED STACK REPLACES YOUR bsdsocket.library, so Roadshow,
AmiTCP and Miami stop working. It also installs its own ping, traceroute, arp,
AddNetInterface, RemoveNetInterface, NetShutdown, GetNetStatus and
ShowNetStatus into C:, over the Roadshow commands of those names. THERE IS NO
UNDO - going back means reinstalling your old stack. Your SANA-II
Ethernet HARDWARE keeps working - the bundled stack drives it, you just
describe it in a DEVS:NetInterfaces/ file instead. What it cannot drive is
non-Ethernet SANA-II: PPP and SLIP dial-up, Token Ring, ArcNet. Note also that
the stack carries ONE interface at a time besides loopback, so it is the Pi's
port or your card, not both.

xhci.device 5.x also runs on Poseidon for AmigaOS 6.x, just without real USB
3.0 or fast UAS storage - so it is the safe answer if you are unsure.

The two '-serial' archives are the same drivers with serial-port diagnostics -
take one only when chasing a problem. Installing a '-rangeops' archive on an
Emu68 without the extensions is safe: the Install script checks, says so, and
stops without changing anything.

Where the advice comes from - measured TCP throughput over gigabit Ethernet,
using genet.device with the bundled lwip-amiga stack:

  official Emu68,             standard archive   104 Mbit/s in,  79 out
  Emu68 with the extensions,  standard archive   698 Mbit/s in, 477 out
  Emu68 with the extensions,  -rangeops archive  near line rate

Those are the netdev driver. Over the SANA-II backend the same stack measures
about 290 Mbit/s in and 300 out: SANA-II copies every packet and cannot offload
checksums, so it gives up most of the netdev gain.


COMPONENTS
----------

gic400.library @GIC400_VERSION@ (LIBS:)
  The interrupt controller library every driver here needs, including
  bcmpcie.library. Installed like any other library in the stack. If your
  Emu68 carries its own copy, that one is already in memory at boot and the
  drivers use it, so the LIBS: file is simply never opened - harmless, and it
  keeps things working on an Emu68 without the library.

bcmpcie.library @BCMPCIE_VERSION@ (LIBS:)
  Talks to the Pi's PCIe bus: brings the link up, finds the devices on it and
  makes them available to the drivers.

xhci.device (DEVS:USBHardware/)
  USB 2.0/3.0 for the Raspberry Pi 4 / CM4. Every USB controller it finds is a
  separate UNIT, and each unit has to be added to your USB stack on its own -
  which units exist depends on your board. See the driver's own README in
  Documentation/emu68-xhci-driver-context/ (or .../-legacy/ for the 5.x
  version) for the unit numbering.
  The driver comes in two versions and you pick ONE at install time (see WHAT
  TO INSTALL above). Poseidon itself is NOT part of this archive.

    6.x @XHCI_VERSION@  for Poseidon for AmigaOS 6.x. Shows USB 3.0 devices as
      what they are instead of disguising them as USB 2.0, and supports the
      fast transfer mode UAS mass storage uses.

    5.x @XHCI_LEGACY_VERSION@  required on classic Poseidon 4.x, and it also
      runs on Poseidon for AmigaOS 6.x - but there SuperSpeed devices appear
      as USB 2.0 and the fast storage mode is not available. It rides in the
      archive under Storage/ and is installed to DEVS:USBHardware/ only if you
      choose it.

genet.device (DEVS:Networks/)
  Gigabit Ethernet for the port built into the Raspberry Pi 4 / CM4. It comes
  in two versions and you pick ONE at install time (see WHAT TO INSTALL above);
  the choice no longer decides your TCP/IP stack:

    netdev  @GENET_VERSION@   the fast zero-copy driver. Only the bundled
      lwip-amiga stack can open it, so Roadshow, AmiTCP and Miami cannot -
      choosing it installs the bundled stack as well. Its netdev-stats
      counter tool installs to C:.

    SANA-II @GENET_SANA2_VERSION@  the classic driver. Works with a third-party
      stack (Roadshow, AmiTCP, Miami) AND with the bundled one, which has
      driven Ethernet SANA-II devices since lwip-amiga 1.4 - slower than
      netdev either way. It rides in the archive under Storage/ and is
      installed to DEVS:Networks/ only if you choose it; its genet-stats
      counter tool then installs to C:.

lwip-amiga @LWIP_VERSION@ (LIBS:bsdsocket.library @BSDSOCKET_VERSION@)
  A modern TCP/IP stack. It provides the standard AmigaOS networking API under
  the usual library name, which is why installing it replaces whatever
  bsdsocket.library you have.
  Opening it starts the stack (loopback only); the network interface is added
  at boot by S:Network-Startup running 'AddNetInterface', from the config file
  DEVS:NetInterfaces/genet (DHCP by default; the file holds a fixed address
  or VLAN too). Stack-wide settings - hostname, DNS servers, mDNS - live in
  ENVARC:netstack.prefs.
  Installing it REPLACES any existing bsdsocket.library, and its commands
  below replace the Roadshow ones of the same name in C:. Neither is undone
  by re-running the installer - reinstall your old stack to go back.
  Besides the netdev genet it also drives classic Ethernet-type SANA-II
  drivers - a Zorro or PCMCIA card, a USB Ethernet adapter - which it detects
  automatically; write a file for one by copying the commented sample from
  SYS:Storage/NetInterfaces/genet and editing DEVICE=. Non-Ethernet SANA-II
  (PPP, SLIP, Token Ring, ArcNet) is not supported.
  ONE interface at a time: besides loopback the stack carries a single
  interface, so a second AddNetInterface is refused. If DEVS:NetInterfaces/
  holds more than one file the boot line picks the highest PRI= tooltype and
  silently skips the rest - keep the spares in SYS:Storage/NetInterfaces/.

netdev-stats (C:)
  Installed with the netdev genet.device: shows that driver's own counters and
  interface statistics live. It speaks the netdev command set directly, so it
  does not work against the SANA-II driver - that one has genet-stats.

netinfo, mdns, GetNetStatus, ShowNetStatus (C:)
  Networking tools installed with the bundled stack. netinfo reports the
  address and DNS settings the stack ended up with; mdns looks up and
  announces .local names; GetNetStatus and ShowNetStatus are the
  Roadshow-compatible status query and report.

AddNetInterface, RemoveNetInterface, NetShutdown, arp (C:)
  Roadshow-style network control, installed with the bundled stack.
  AddNetInterface adds interfaces from DEVS:NetInterfaces/ files (blocking
  until the DHCP lease arrives); RemoveNetInterface takes one down again
  (FORCE overrides the in-use refusal); NetShutdown stops the whole stack
  and unloads the library once every network program has quit; Arp displays,
  sets and deletes ARP table entries (published/proxy entries are not
  supported).

ping, traceroute (C:)
  The classic 4.4BSD network diagnostics, installed with the bundled stack,
  with Roadshow-compatible command templates. ping sends ICMP echo requests
  and reports round-trip times and packet loss; traceroute maps the gateways
  a packet crosses on its way to a host (ping's RECORDROUTE option is not
  supported by this stack).

NetLogViewer (C:, with icon)
  Log viewer, installed with the bundled stack: a Commodity
  that captures every message the stack and its clients log
  (interface and link state, DHCP leases, errors, syslog() output) and shows
  them in a window with time, origin and severity; the list can be saved to
  a file. Shift-Alt-F8 (CX_POPKEY) shows the window, Exchange hides and
  shows it, NetShutdown makes it exit. Start it before AddNetInterface to
  see the whole bring-up, for example from S:Network-Startup with
  "Run >NIL: C:NetLogViewer CX_POPUP NO". The icon carries the CX_POPKEY /
  CX_PRIORITY / CX_POPUP tooltypes and DONOTWAIT, so a copy in WBStartup
  works too.

nvme.device @NVME_VERSION@ (DEVS:)
  Storage driver for a PCIe-attached NVMe SSD.

nvmeinfo, nvmeadm (C:)
  Tools for nvme.device, installed automatically with the driver. nvmeinfo
  lists the drives and their namespaces; nvmeadm reports SMART/health data,
  log pages, self-test results and firmware information.

openpci.library @OPENPCI_VERSION@ (LIBS:) [optional]
  A compatibility shim for older software that opens "openpci.library" by
  name; it forwards those calls to bcmpcie.library. Do NOT install it if you
  have a real PCI expansion board (Mediator, Prometheus, G-REX, ...).

lspci (C:) [optional]
  Lists the devices found on the PCIe bus. Run it from a Shell to check the
  link is up and everything was detected.


INSTALLATION
------------

Run the included 'Install' script using the Commodore Installer tool
(v43.3 or later, included with AmigaOS 3.1.4+).

Pick the components you want from the checklist, then answer three questions:
ONE USB version (xhci 6.x, xhci 5.x, or none), ONE Ethernet driver (netdev
genet, SANA-II genet, or none) and - unless you picked netdev, which answers
it for you - which TCP/IP stack (the bundled lwip-amiga, or leave
bsdsocket.library alone). The installer pulls in the libraries they need
automatically, so you cannot end up with a driver whose library is missing,
and it never downgrades a newer copy already on your system - if an equal or
newer version is installed, it asks first. Three things are always replaced,
because switching versions keeps the same filename and a version check would
refuse: the chosen xhci.device, the chosen genet.device and, when you choose
the bundled stack, bsdsocket.library.

If you are upgrading from 2.0 the installer shows one extra screen, telling
you that the interface settings in your ENVARC:netstack.prefs are no longer
read. It changes nothing - see AFTER INSTALLATION for what to move where.

On the -rangeops archives the Install script first checks that your Emu68 has
the dcache extensions and refuses to continue if it does not. If you are
preparing a disk on a machine that is not the target, it warns and lets you
carry on. The small tool it uses for the check runs from the unpacked archive
and is never copied to C:.

Manual installation: copy files to the destinations listed above and reboot.


AFTER INSTALLATION
------------------

Reboot, then finish the steps the drivers cannot do for themselves. The
installer's closing screen lists the ones that apply to your selection; the
full set is:

  xhci 6.x   Install Poseidon for AmigaOS 6.x (distributed separately), then
             add EACH xhci.device unit as a USB host controller in Trident,
             or from a Shell with:
               AddUSBHardware xhci.device <unit>
             The driver's README in Documentation/ lists the units.

  xhci 5.x   Install a Poseidon USB stack, then add EACH xhci.device unit as a
             USB host controller in Trident. On Poseidon for AmigaOS 6.x the
             6.x version is the faster one - re-run the installer to switch.

  bundled    S:Network-Startup adds the network interface at boot with
  stack      AddNetInterface (DHCP; per-interface settings live in
             DEVS:NetInterfaces/genet, stack-wide ones in
             ENVARC:netstack.prefs). netinfo and ShowNetStatus show what the
             stack ended up with; NetShutdown stops the network again.
             To use a Zorro/PCMCIA card or a USB Ethernet adapter instead of
             the Pi's port, copy SYS:Storage/NetInterfaces/genet into
             DEVS:NetInterfaces/ under a name of your choosing, set DEVICE=
             (and UNIT=) to your driver, and remove the genet file - only
             one interface can be up at a time.

  SANA-II    If you kept your own TCP/IP stack, point it (Roadshow, AmiTCP,
  + own      Miami) at 'genet.device' unit 0 before it can carry traffic.
  stack      genet-stats shows the driver's counters.

  netdev     netdev-stats shows the driver's own counters and loss points.

  upgrading  Interface settings from a 2.0 ENVARC:netstack.prefs are ignored
  from 2.0   now; move them into DEVS:NetInterfaces/genet, which the
             installer created for you:
               MODE=STATIC + ADDRESS/NETMASK/GATEWAY  ->  a dotted-quad
                 ADDRESS= plus NETMASK= (and GATEWAY= if you had one)
               DEVICE, UNIT, VLAN                     ->  same keys, same
                 values, just in the interface file
             DHCP setups need nothing - that is the default. HOSTNAME, DNS1,
             DNS2, MDNS and NETWORK stay in netstack.prefs and still work.

  nvme       nvmeinfo lists the NVMe drives and namespaces that were found;
             nvmeadm reports SMART/health data and log pages.

  gic400     Nothing to do. If your Emu68 supplies gic400.library from its own
             ROM, that copy is already in memory at boot and the drivers use
             it, so the LIBS: copy simply goes unused.


REQUIREMENTS
------------

  - Raspberry Pi 4 or CM4 running @CACHE_OPS_FIRMWARE_REQ@
  - AmigaOS 3.x
  - USB stack (for xhci.device): Poseidon, distributed separately. The 6.x
    driver needs Poseidon for AmigaOS 6.x; the 5.x driver runs on classic
    Poseidon 4.x and on 6.x alike
  - Networking (for genet.device): the netdev version requires the bundled
    lwip-amiga stack; the SANA-II version works with either that or a
    third-party stack such as Roadshow, AmiTCP or Miami. The bundled stack
    needs no genet at all if you connect through an Ethernet-type SANA-II
    card or USB adapter


